(I) Proposition 6.5: one function for a family of decision rules (coin P0, uniform mu on u inputs)
==============================================================================
  u=16 eta=0.20: Pr_F[some rule deviates beyond its bound] = 0.0042 (<= eta); |E_F a_F - a0| = ['0.0312', '0.0000', '0.0156'] <= C(m,2)/u = ['0.0625', '0.0000', '0.0625']
     bounds used: ['0.715', '0.326', '0.715']
  u=400 eta=0.10: Pr_F[some rule deviates beyond its bound] = 0.0032 (<= eta); |E_F a_F - a0| = ['0.0012', '0.0000', '0.0006'] <= C(m,2)/u = ['0.0025', '0.0000', '0.0025']
     bounds used: ['0.146', '0.072', '0.146']

==============================================================================
(J) Theorem 6.6: one input asked r times; exact total variation of the r answers
==============================================================================
  (s, r) = (4, 2): exact TV = 0.25000, bound C(r,2)/s = 0.25000, within: True
  (s, r) = (4, 3): exact TV = 0.37500, bound C(r,2)/s = 0.75000, within: True
  (s, r) = (256, 2): exact TV = 0.00391, bound C(r,2)/s = 0.00391, within: True
  (s, r) = (256, 8): exact TV = 0.09543, bound C(r,2)/s = 0.10938, within: True
  (s, r) = (65536, 128): exact TV = 0.10322, bound C(r,2)/s = 0.12402, within: True

==============================================================================
(K) Theorem 6.6(b): adaptive procedures on 2 inputs, s = 4, m = 2 (all) and m = 3 (random sample)
==============================================================================
  m=2: max advantage over all 32 deterministic adaptive procedures = 1/4 = 0.2500, bound C(2,2)/s = 0.2500
  m=3: max advantage over 401 procedures (the constant one and random adaptive ones) = 0.3750, bound C(3,2)/s = 0.7500
